          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebra"} von Neumann algebra
            write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A'
            for its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutant"} commutant
            in the ambient algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(\mathcal{H})
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+operators"} bounded operators
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A von Neumann algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is called a
              %strong factor
              if its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/center"} center
              is trivial, equivalently if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutant"} commutant
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A'
              generate the full algebra of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+operators"} bounded operators
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℋ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(\mathcal{H})
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi A
                    %mo ′
                    %mo =
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Z(A)
                    \\coloneqq
                    A \cap A'
                    \=
                    \\mathbb{C}1
                    \\,.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            Every von Neumann algebra may be written as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+integral"} direct integral
            over factors. (
            %a{:href => "#vNeumann49"}> von Neumann 1849
            )
          %h2#classification Classification
          %p
            Factors are classified in terms of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            of their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            of finite
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                W*-modules
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/W%5C%2A-modules"} ?
            %span.newWikiWord
              W*-module
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/W%5C%2A-module"} ?
            over a factor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is
            %em finite
            if it is not isomorphic to its proper submodule.
          %h3#type_i Type I
          %p
            Type I factors are characterized by the condition that the K-theory of finite modules is isomorphic to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Z}
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(H)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
          %h3#type_ii Type II
          %p
            Type II factors are characterized by the condition that the K-theory of finite modules is isomorphic to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{R}
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
          %p
            Type II factors are subdivided into two classes: type II
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} _1
            factors are characterized by the condition that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a finite
            = succeed "-module," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} _\infty
            factor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is not a finite
            = succeed "-module." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %h3#TypeIII Type III
          %p Type III factors are characterized by the condition that the K-theory of finite modules is trivial, i.e., only the zero module is finite.
          %p
            Type III factors are further subdivided into three classes, according to the structure of the center of their
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                modular algebra
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/modular+algebra"} ?
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/imaginary+numbers"} imaginary numbers
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                noncommutative L^p-spaces
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/noncommutative+L%5Ep-spaces"} ?
          %p
            By the
            %span.newWikiWord
              von Neumann duality
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/von+Neumann+duality"} ?
            for commutative von Neumann algebras, the spectrum of this center is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+space"} measurable space
            equipped with a σ-ideal of negligible sets and the grading yields an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+action"} action
            of the additive group of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{R}
            = succeed "." do
              %em noncommutative flow of weights
          %p
            If the center is trivial (so that the spectrum is a point), then the factor has type III
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} _1
            \. If the action of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{R}
            is not periodic, then the factor has type III
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} _0
            \. If the action is periodic with period
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo
                      %mrow
                        %mi exp
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mi λ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} _{\exp(-\lambda)}
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"} C-star algebra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniformly+hyperfinite+algebra"} uniformly hyperfinite algebra
          %h2#references References
          %h3#ReferencesGeneral General
          %p The original articles:
          %p The classification of factors into types I, II, III and the construction of examples not of type I:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+J.+Murray"}> Francis J. Murray
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+von+Neumann"} John von Neumann
              = succeed "," do
                %em On Rings of Operators
              %strong 37
              1 (1936) 116-229 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2307/1968693"}> doi:10.2307/1968693
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/1968693"} jstor:1968693
          %p
            Discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/traces"} traces
            on these factors:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+J.+Murray"}> Francis J. Murray
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+von+Neumann"} John von Neumann
              = succeed "," do
                %em On rings of operators. II
              %strong 41
              (1937) 208-248 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2307/1989620"}> doi:10.2307/1989620
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/1989620"} jstor:1989620
          %p
            On
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            of factors and proof of a single isomorphism class of approximately finite type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi II
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} II_1
            factors:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+J.+Murray"}> Francis J. Murray
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+von+Neumann"} John von Neumann
              = succeed "," do
                %em On rings of operators. IV
              %strong 44
              4 (1943) 716-808 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2307/1969107"}> doi:10.2307/1969107
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/1969107"} jstor:1969107
          %p
            On decomposing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebras"} von Neumann algebras
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+integral"} direct integral
            of factors:
          %ul
            %li#vNeumann49
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+von+Neumann"}> John von Neumann
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On rings of operators, reduction theory
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.jstor.org/stable/1969463"} jstor:1969463
          %p The classification of type III factors:
          %ul
            %li#Connes75
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Structure Theory for Type III Factors
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://cm2vivi2002.free.fr/AC-biblio/AC-biblio26.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Recollection of the history which made von Neumann turn to discussion of these “factors”, motivated from considerations in the foundations of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic"} quantum logic
          %ul
            %li{:id => "Rédei96"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Miklos+R%C3%A9dei"}> Miklos Rédei
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Why John von Neumann did not Like the Hilbert Space formalism of quantum mechanics (and what he liked instead)
              %strong 27
              4 (1996) 493-510 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/S1355-2198(96)00017-2"}> doi:10.1016/S1355-2198(96)00017-2
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p Lecture notes:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vaikalathur+S.+Sunder"}> Vaikalathur S. Sunder
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    von Neumann algebras,
                    = succeed "-factors," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi II
                              %mn 1
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} II_1
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.imsc.res.in/~sunder/iitb3.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Sunder-vNSubfactors.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                Hideki Kosaki,
                %em Type III factors and index theory
                (1993) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://pages.uoregon.edu/njp/lec-f.pdf"}> pdf
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            Exposition with an eye towards discussion of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entanglement+entropy"} entanglement entropy
          %ul
            %li
              Jonathan Sorce,
              %em Notes on the type classification of von Neumann algebras
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.01958"}> arXiv:2302.01958
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %h3#subfactors Subfactors
          %p
            The mathematics of inclusions of
            %em subfactors
            is giving deep structural insights. See also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/planar+algebra"} planar algebra
          %p Monograph:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vaughan+F.+R.+Jones"}> Vaughan F. R. Jones
              ,
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vaikalathur+S.+Sunder"} Vaikalathur S. Sunder
              = succeed "," do
                %em Introduction to Subfactors
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511566219"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511566219
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vaughan+Jones"}> Vaughan Jones
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Index for subfactors
                %strong 72
                (1983) 1–25 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01389127"}> doi;10.1007/BF01389127
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://eudml.org/doc/143011"} eudml:143011
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vaughan+Jones"}> Vaughan Jones
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A polynomial invariant for links via von Neumann algebras
                %strong 12
                (1985) 103-111 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/journals/bulletin-of-the-american-mathematical-society-new-series/volume-12/issue-1/A-polynomial-invariant-for-knots-via-von-Neumann-algebras/bams/1183552338.full"}> euclid:bams/1183552338
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vaughan+Jones"}> Vaughan Jones
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Hecke algebra representations of braid groups and link polynomials
                %strong 126
                335 (1987)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vaughan+Jones"}> Vaughan Jones
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Scott+Morrison"} Scott Morrison
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noah+Snyder"} Noah Snyder
                %em The classification of subfactors of index at most 5
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1304.6141"}> arXiv:1304.6141
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vaughan+F.+R.+Jones"}> Vaughan F. R. Jones
                , David Penneys:
                %em Infinite index subfactors and the GICAR categories
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1410.0856"}> arxiv/1410.0856
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            Symmetries of depth two inclusions of subfactors may be described via associative
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bialgebroid"} bialgebroid
          %ul
            %li
              Lars Kadison, Kornél Szlachányi,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Bialgebroid actions on depth two extensions and duality
              = succeed ":1" do
                %strong 179
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/S0001-8708(02)00028-2"} doi
          %h3#ReferencesInQuantumFieldTheory Relation to quantum field theory
          %p
            On von Neumann algebra factors as algebras of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            (which was their original motivation, cf.
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#Rédei96"} Rédei 1996
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jakob+Yngvason"}> Jakob Yngvason
                ,
                %em The role of type III factors in quantum field theory
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/0411058"}> arXiv:math-ph/0411058
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jonathan+Sorce"}> Jonathan Sorce
                ,
